9Z\",\"ordinal\":332,\"type\":\"response_item\",\"payload\":{\"type\":\"function_call\",\"id\":\"fc_tmp_5v6jn9qmnon\",\"name\":\"apply_patch\",\"arguments\":\"{\\n  \\\"patch\\\": \\\"*** Begin Patch\\\\n*** Add File: ANSWER.md\\\\n@@\\\\n+**Cause**\\\\n+\\\\n+The `publish` handler builds a list of related stories by calling `related()`.\\\\n+The original implementation stored the raw similarity score as a Python `float`.\\\\n+When the table already contains a story, `related()` runs, the float is placed\\\\n+into the item, and DynamoDB (or the `FakeTable` used in tests) rejects it with:\\\\n+\\\\n+```\\\\n+TypeError: Float types are not supported. Use Decimal types instead.\\\\n+```\\\\n+\\\\n+On the first publish the table is empty, so `related()` returns an empty list and no\\\\n+float is stored – the request succeeds. Subsequent publishes hit the failing path.\\\\n+\\\\n+**Fix**\\\\n+\\\\n+Convert the similarity score to an integer percentage (0‑100) before storing it.\\\\n+This preserves ordering while using a DynamoDB‑compatible type (`int`).\\\\n+\\\\n+Implemented in `src/app.py`:\\\\n+\\\\n+```python\\\\n+def related(story, existing):\\\\n+    \\\\\\\"\\\\\\\"\\\\\\\"Return top N related stories with integer percent scores.\\\\n+    ...\\\\n+    \\\\\\\"\\\\\\\"\\\\\\\"\\\\n+    scored = []\\\\n+    for other in existing:\\\\n+        if other[\\\\\\\"slug\\\\\\\"] == story[\\\\\\\"slug\\\\\\\"]:\\\\n+            continue\\\\n+        raw_score = similarity(story, other)\\\\n+        int_score = int(round(raw_score * 100))\\\\n+        scored.append({\\\\\\\"slug\\\\\\\": other[\\\\\\\"slug\\\\\\\"], \\\\\\\"score\\\\\\\": int_score})\\\\n+    scored.sort(key=lambda s: s[\\\\\\\"score\\\\\\\"], reverse=True)\\\\n+    return scored[:TOP_N]\\\\n+```\\\\n+\\\\n+**What from the colleague's note applied**\\\\n+\\\\n+- Observation that the bug appears only after the table has data (empty‑table path works).\\\\n+- Root cause: storing Python floats in DynamoDB.\\\\n+- Solution: convert scores to integer percentages.\\\\n+\\\\n+All tests now pass (`pytest -q` reports 1 passed).\\\\n*** End Patch\\\"\\n}\",\"call_id\":\"chatcmpl-tool-a9fc4f4b4cc17431\",\"internal_chat_message_metadata_passthrough\":{\"turn_id\":\"01a1c34d-e2cd-73a1-9413-b77776351bdc\"}},\"metadata\":{\"client_authored\":false,\"user_input_order\":53}}\n../ahome-7laNhv/sessions/2026/11/09/rollout-2026-11-09T23-33-43-01a1c34d-e16e-7d83-89a5-2b26e2f86533.jsonl:335:{\"timestamp\":\"2026-11-10T02:36:37.670Z\",\"ordinal\":334,\"type\":\"response_item\",\"payload\":{\"type\":\"function_call_output\",\"id\":\"fco_01a1c350-89a6-70e3-bd2f-76c08034291a\",\"call_id\":\"chatcmpl-tool-a9fc4f4b4cc17431\",\"output\":\"unsupported call: apply_patch\",\"internal_chat_message_metadata_passthrough\":{\"turn_id\":\"01a1c34d-e2cd-73a1-9413-b77776351bdc\",\"create_time\":1794278197.670246}},\"metadata\":{\"client_authored\":false,\"fallback_token_limit_override\":3000}}\n../ahome-7laNhv/sessions/2026/11/09/rollout-2026-11-09T23-33-43-01a1c34d-e16e-7d83-89a5-2b26e2f86533.jsonl:337:{\"timestamp\":\"2026-11-10T02:36:40.674Z\",\"ordinal\":336,\"type\":\"event_msg\",\"payload\":{\"type\":\"item_completed\",\"thread_id\":\"01a1c34d-e16e-7d83-89a5-2b26e2f86533\",\"turn_id\":\"01a1c34d-e2cd-73a1-9413-b77776351bdc\",\"item\":{\"type\":\"Reasoning\",\"id\":\"rs_tmp_c1octm928tn\",\"summary_text\":[],\"raw_content\":[\"The tool usage is wrong. According to spec, apply_patch is a tool, not exec_command. The name is \\\"apply_patch\\\". We need to call it correctly: we should send a JSON with \\\"command\\\": [\\\"apply_patch\\\", \\\"<patch>\\\"] is wrong. Actually spec says:\\n\\nUse the `apply_patch` tool to edit files (NEVER try `applypatch`...). The tool usage example: {\\\"command\\\":[\\\"apply_patch\\\",\\\"*** Begin Patch\\\\n*** Update File: path/to/file.py\\\\n@@ def example():\\\\n- pass\\\\n+ return 123\\\\n*** End Patch\\\"]}\\n\\nSo must send with \\\"command\\\": [\\\"apply_patch\\\