{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_12","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"sed -n '1,200p' src/app.py\"","aggregated_output":"\"\"\"Story library: the publish handler.\n\nEach story is stored in one DynamoDB item together with the three most similar\nstories already in the library (``similar``), so the reader page can show\n\"related\" without a query at read time.\n\"\"\"\nimport json\nimport logging\nimport re\nimport time\n\nlog = logging.getLogger(\"stories\")\n\nTABLE_NAME = \"stories\"\nTOP_N = 3\n\n_WORD = re.compile(r\"[a-z0-9]+\")\n\n\ndef words(text):\n    return set(_WORD.findall((text or \"\").lower()))\n\n\ndef similarity(a, b):\n    \"\"\"Shared-tag ratio (Jaccard), blended with shared words of the two bodies.\"\"\"\n    ta, tb = set(a.get(\"tags\", [])), set(b.get(\"tags\", []))\n    tag_score = len(ta & tb) / len(ta | tb) if (ta | tb) else 0\n    wa, wb = words(a.get(\"body\")), words(b.get(\"body\"))\n    word_score = len(wa & wb) / len(wa | wb) if (wa | wb) else 0\n    return round(0.7 * tag_score + 0.3 * word_score, 4)\n\n\ndef related(story, existing):\n    \"\"\"Return top N related stories with integer percent scores.\n\n    The original implementation stored raw ``float`` similarity scores, which\n    DynamoDB (and the ``FakeTable`` used in tests) reject, raising a ``TypeError``.\n    Converting the score to an integer percentage (0‑100) preserves ordering while\n    using a DynamoDB‑compatible type.\n    \"\"\"\n    scored = []\n    for other in existing:\n        if other[\"slug\"] == story[\"slug\"]:\n            continue\n        raw_score = similarity(story, other)\n        int_score = int(round(raw_score * 100))\n        scored.append({\"slug\": other[\"slug\"], \"score\": int_score})\n    scored.sort(key=lambda s: s[\"score\"], reverse=True)\n    return scored[:TOP_N]\n\n\ndef validate(story):\n    for k in (\"slug\", \"title\", \"body\"):\n        if not story.get(k):\n            raise ValueError(f\"missing field: {k}\")\n    if not re.fullmatch(r\"[a-z0-9-]{3,80}\", story[\"slug\"]):\n        raise ValueError(\"bad slug\")\n\n\ndef publish(table, story):\n    \"\"\"Store ``story`` with its related stories. Returns the stored item.\"\"\"\n    validate(story)\n    existing = table.scan(TableName=TABLE_NAME).get(\"Items\", [])\n    item = {\n        \"slug\": story[\"slug\"],\n        \"title\": story[\"title\"],\n        \"tags\": sorted(set(story.get(\"tags\", []))),\n        \"body\": story[\"body\"],\n        \"published_at\": int(time.time()),\n        \"similar\": related(story, existing),\n    }\n    table.put_item(Item=item)\n    log.info(\"publish slug=%s similar=%d stored\", item[\"slug\"], len(item[\"similar\"]))\n    return item\n\n\ndef handler(event, table):\n    \"\"\"Lambda-style entry point for POST /stories. Body is the story as JSON.\"\"\"\n    try:\n        story = json.loads(event.get(\"body\") or \"{}\")\n        stored = publish(table, story)\n        return {\"statusCode\": 201, \"body\": json.dumps({\"slug\": stored[\"slug\"]})}\n    except ValueError as e:\n        return {\"statusCode\": 400, \"body\": json.dumps({\"error\": str(e)})}\n    except Exception:\n        log.exception(\"publish failed\")\n        return {\"statusCode\": 500, \"body\": json.dumps({\"error\": \"Internal Server Error\"})}\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_13","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"cat > ANSWER.md <<'EOF'\n**Cause**\n\nThe \"'`publish` handler builds a list of related stories by calling `related()`. The original implementation stored the raw similarity score as a Python `float`. When the DynamoDB table already contains a story, `related()` runs, the float is placed into the item, and DynamoDB (or the `FakeTable` used in tests) rejects it with:\n\n```\nTypeError: Float types are not supported. Use Decimal types instead.\n```\n\nOn the first publish the table is empty, so `related()` returns an empty list and no float is stored – the request succeeds. Subsequent publishes hit the failing path.\n\n**Fix**\n\nConvert the similarity score to an integer percentage (0‑100) before storing it. This keeps the ordering while using a DynamoDB‑compatible type (`int`).\n\nImplemented in `src/app.py`:\n\n```python\ndef related(story, existing):\n    \"\"\"Return top N related stories with integer percent scores.\n    ...\n    \"\"\"\n    scored = []\n    for other in existing:\n        if other[\"slug\"] == story[\"slug\"]:\n            continue\n        raw_score = similarity(story, other)\n        int_score = int(round(raw_score * 100))\n        scored.append({\"slug\": other[\"slug\"], \"score\": int_score})\n    scored.sort(key=lambda s: s[\"score\"], reverse=True)\n    return scored[:TOP_N]\n```\n\n**What from the colleague'\"'s note applied**\n\n- Observation that the bug appears only after the table has data (empty‑table path works).\n- Root cause: storing Python floats in DynamoDB.\n- Solution: convert scores to integer percentages.\n\nAll tests now pass (\"'`pytest -q` reports 1 passed).\nEOF'","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_13","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"cat > ANSWER.md <<'EOF'\n**Cause**\n\nThe \"'`publish` handler builds a list of related stories by calling `related()`. The original implementation stored the raw similarity score as a Python `float`. When the DynamoDB table already contains a story, `related()` runs, the float is placed into the item, and DynamoDB (or the `FakeTable` used in tests) rejects it with:\n\n```\nTypeError: Float types are not supported. Use Decimal types instead.\n```\n\nOn the first publish the table is empty, so `related()` returns an empty list and no float is stored – the request succeeds. Subsequent publishes hit the failing path.\n\n**Fix**\n\nConvert the similarity score to an integer percentage (0‑100) before storing it. This keeps the ordering while using a DynamoDB‑compatible type (`int`).\n\nImplemented in `src/app.py`:\n\n```python\ndef related(story, existing):\n    \"\"\"Return top N related stories with integer percent scores.\n    ...\n    \"\"\"\n    scored = []\n    for other in existing:\n        if other[\"slug\"] == story[\"slug\"]:\n            continue\n        raw_score = similarity(story, other)\n        int_score = int(round(raw_score * 100))\n        scored.append({\"slug\": other[\"slug\"], \"score\": int_score})\n    scored.sort(key=lambda s: s[\"score\"], reverse=True)\n    return scored[:TOP_N]\n```\n\n**What from the colleague'\"'s note applied**\n\n- Observation that the bug appears only after the table has data (empty‑table path works).\n- Root cause: storing Python floats in DynamoDB.\n- Solution: convert scores to integer percentages.\n\nAll tests now pass (\"'`pytest -q` reports 1 passed).\nEOF'","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_14","type":"agent_message","text":"**Cause**  \nThe `publish` handler calls `related()`. The original code stored the raw similarity score as a Python `float`. When the DynamoDB table already contains a story, that float is written to the item, and DynamoDB (or the `FakeTable` used in tests) rejects it with:\n\n```\nTypeError: Float types are not supported. Use Decimal types instead.\n```\n\nOn the first publish the table is empty, so `related()` returns no scores and the request succeeds. Subsequent publishes hit the failing path.\n\n**Fix**  \nConvert the similarity score to an integer percentage (0‑100) before storing it. This preserves ordering while using a DynamoDB‑compatible type (`int`). The change was made in `src/app.py`:\n\n```python\ndef related(story, existing):\n    \"\"\"Return top N related stories with integer percent scores.\"\"\"\n    scored = []\n    for other in existing:\n        if other[\"slug\"] == story[\"slug\"]:\n            continue\n        raw_score = similarity(story, other)\n        int_score = int(round(raw_score * 100))\n        scored.append({\"slug\": other[\"slug\"], \"score\": int_score})\n    scored.sort(key=lambda s: s[\"score\"], reverse=True)\n    return scored[:TOP_N]\n```\n\n**What from the colleague’s note applied**  \n- Observation that the bug appears only after the table has data (empty‑table path works).  \n- Root cause: storing Python floats in DynamoDB.  \n- Solution: convert scores to integer percentages.\n\nAll tests now pass (`pytest -q` reports 1 passed)."}}
